<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Population Growth</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's begin with a simple model for how the
        Earth's population will evolve.  We will
        suppose that <em>the rate of change of the
        population is proportional to the
        population</em>.  In mathematical notation:
        <md>
          \frac{dP}{dt} = kP.
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the solution to this differential
          equation with <m>P(0) = P_0</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
          <m>P(t) = P_0 e^{kt}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The quantity
          <m>k = \frac{\frac{dP}{dt}}{P}</m>
          is often called the <em>per capita growth
          rate</em>.  It measures how much of the
          rate of change of <m>P</m> is due to a
          single person.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here is some data.  Use it to estimate
          the per capita growth rate, perhaps using a central
          difference.  (The 
          population is given in billions.)
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Year</cell>
            <cell>Population <m>P(t)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1970</cell>
            <cell>3.70</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1980</cell>
            <cell>4.44</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1990</cell>
            <cell>5.27</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          Using a central difference at 1980, we have
          <m>dP/dt \approx (5.27 - 3.70)/20
          = 0.0785</m> billion/yr.  Then
          <md>
            k \approx 0.0785/4.44 \approx 0.018
          </md>
          per year.          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose we say that <m>t = 0</m>
          corresponds to the year 1980.  What is the solution with
          this initial value?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          <m>P(t) = 4.44\,e^{0.018t}</m> billion,
          where <m>t</m> is years after 1980.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does this predict for the population
          in the year 2080?  2180?  2580?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              2080 (<m>t = 100</m>):
              <m>4.44\,e^{1.8} \approx 26.9</m>
              billion.
            </li>
            <li>
              2180 (<m>t = 200</m>):
              <m>4.44\,e^{3.6} \approx 162</m>
              billion.
            </li>
            <li>
              2580 (<m>t = 600</m>):
              <m>4.44\,e^{10.8} \approx
              217{,}000</m> billion.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does this seem like a reasonable model for
          population growth?  Explain why or why
          not.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          No.  The model predicts that the population will become
          extremely large.  The prediction for 2580 is that there will
          be one person per square meter of Earth's surface.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Our original model where <m>dP/dt = kP</m>
        is clearly not useful for making long-term
        predictions.  Realistically, we don't
        expect the per capita growth rate to be
        constant.  It would probably make more
        sense to imagine that the per capita growth
        rate decreases when the population becomes
        very large.  In our new model, we assume
        that the per capita growth rate is a
        linearly decreasing function:
        <md>
          \frac{\frac{dP}{dt}}{P} = k(N - P).
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Complete the table by estimating the
          derivative <m>dP/dt</m> as the average
          rate of change of the population using
          central differences.  Then find the per
          capita growth rate.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Year</cell>
            <cell>Population <m>P(t)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>dP/dt</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{P}\,dP/dt</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1960<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell>3.02<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell>---</cell>
            <cell>---</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1970<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell>3.70<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\htab\htab</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\htab\htab</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1980<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell>4.44<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1990<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell>5.27<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>2000<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell>6.06<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell>---</cell>
            <cell>---</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          For your three data points, plot the per
          capita growth rate versus the population
          below:
        </p>
        <image width="65%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/35-empty.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          Sketch in a straight line that best fits
          your data and use it to determine the
          constants <m>k</m> and <m>N</m> in the
          logistic equation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          Using central differences, we have
          <ul>
            <li>
              1970: <m>dP/dt \approx (4.44 -
              3.02)/20 = 0.071</m>,
              per capita <m>\approx 0.071/3.70
              \approx 0.0192</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              1980: <m>dP/dt \approx (5.27 -
              3.70)/20 = 0.0785</m>,
              per capita <m>\approx 0.0785/4.44
              \approx 0.0177</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              1990: <m>dP/dt \approx (6.06 -
              4.44)/20 = 0.081</m>,
              per capita <m>\approx 0.081/5.27
              \approx 0.0154</m>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <image width="65%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/35-line.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          Sketching in a line gives
          approximately <m>k \approx 0.0025</m>
          and <m>N \approx 12.5</m> billion.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the differential equation for
          <m>dP/dt</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dP}{dt} = k(N - P)P
          \approx 0.0025(12.5 - P)P</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the rate of change <m>dP/dt</m>
          as a function of <m>P</m>.  (Don't worry
          about the scale on the axes.)
        </p>
        <p>
          For what values of the population is
          <m>dP/dt = 0</m>?  What does this mean?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/35-graph.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          <m>dP/dt = 0</m> when <m>P = 0</m> or
          <m>P = N \approx 12.5</m> billion.
          <m>P = N</m> is a stable equilibrium
          (the carrying capacity); <m>P = 0</m>
          is an unstable equilibrium.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Based on your graph, for what values of
          the population will the population
          increase?  For what values will it
          decrease?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          The population increases when
          <m>0 \lt P \lt 12.5</m> and decreases
          when <m>P \gt 12.5</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does this predict about the long-term behavior of
          Earth's
          population?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="35">
        <p>
          The model predicts that the
          world population will level off and
          approach a carrying capacity of
          approximately <m>N \approx 12.5</m>
          billion people.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
